abstract:
&lt;div&gt;
 Problem Definition: Emergency department (ED) physicians treat patients&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;with different symptoms and constantly switch between tasks. Utilizing three years of&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;comprehensive data on patient visits and lab tests from two large EDs, we investigate&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;the impact of task switching on physician productivity, quality of care, and patient&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;routing. We subsequently provide operational solutions based on the findings.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Methodology: To address estimation bias due to measurement errors and endogenous&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;patient selection, we focus on the beginning of shifts when patients’ treatment time&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;is measured with the highest precision, and exploit an instrumental variable strategy&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;based on the exogenous composition of waiting patients. Given heterogeneous switching&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;costs across task sequences, we leverage a max bisection algorithm that partitions&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;patients into two clusters to minimize the negative impact of task switching.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;span&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Results: On average, a one-standard-deviation increase in the frequency of switching&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;(0.281) reduces the number of patients per hour (PPH) by about 25.6%. However, we&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;find no significant influence on treatment quality. We propose a data-driven queue&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;management method to optimally partition patients into two queues. Based on simulations&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;of the proposed two-queue system in our collaborating EDs, we find that the&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;average waiting time is reduced by up to 26%.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 &lt;br&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;div&gt;
 Managerial Implications: Task switching negatively impacts ED physician productivity,&amp;nbsp;&lt;span&gt;and this impact is more prominent for certain patient type pairs. Recognizing&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;these switching costs, we propose measures to mitigate switching costs, which can&amp;nbsp;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span&gt;considerably reduce ED congestion and patient waiting times.&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This study challenges the universal “best time to post” heuristic by arguing that online communities exhibit distinct, measurable temporal signatures. We test whether the delay between information seeking (Google Search) and social engagement (Reddit) is random noise or a structural manifestation of collective cognitive processing. The goal is to explain why identical timing strategies succeed in some domains yet fail in others, and to replace one‑size‑fits‑all calendars with community‑specific timing that respects cognitive processing speeds and cultural rhythms. Methodologically, we conceptualize communities as damped harmonic oscillators, bridging physical signal processing with social theory. Using nine years of high‑frequency data from eight heterogeneous Reddit communities (N=125,432 posts) cross‑validated with contemporaneous Google Trends, we estimate dominant cycles via spectral analysis and quantify cross‑platform alignment with lead–lag correlation. A community‑level synchronization metric classifies behaviors and recovers incubation intervals. Robustness checks include windowing choices, detrending, surrogate tests for serial dependence, and sensitivity to overlap windows in Trends stitching.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Our findings uncover a three‑cluster typology shaped by community‑specific cognitive incubation: i) Synchronized communities show near‑instant coupling between search and discussion; ii) Phase‑shifted communities exhibit a consistent processing delay before conversation emerges; iii) Autonomous communities follow engagement rhythms largely independent of external search trends. The typology is stable across years and insensitive to reasonable preprocessing choices. Apparent “silence” frequently reflects a functional incubation gap rather than disinterest, explaining intra‑platform variance in content timing performance. While we rely on publicly available Reddit and Google Trends signals where platform policy shifts may introduce unobserved confounds, the lead–lag structure remains robust.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Theoretically, we extend Media Synchronicity Theory from medium‑level affordances to community‑level norms, motivating formal models of collective cognitive processing time. Practically, timing is redefined as a strategic variable rather than a universal constant. Managers should align cadence to community processing speed: real‑time automation for synchronized domains; “prime–wait–engage” sequences for phase‑shifted domains; and culture‑centric programming for autonomous communities. Socially, respecting incubation periods can improve discourse quality by reducing knee‑jerk posting and encouraging deliberation. We introduce community‑specific temporal signatures and a validated three‑cluster typology, reframing silence as a functional incubation gap and offering actionable guidance for campaign design and real‑time optimization.&lt;/p&gt;
